/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch32_fast_intermediate_full -- a reactive intermediate, every rate
  integrated:

      A  --k1-->  I  --k2-->  P      k1 = 0.001 1/s, k2 = 0.1 1/s

  (I is compB and P is compC.)  Isothermal at 350 K, first order, Ea = 0.

  THE QUESTION.  I is used up a hundred times faster than it is made, so it
  never accumulates: after a short first moment its amount is whatever
  makes its two rates equal, n_I = (k1/k2) n_A, small and following A.
  Then P is made as fast as A is consumed:

      dn_P/dt = k1 n_A

  That is the quasi-steady intermediate, and
  batch33_fast_intermediate_reduced runs exactly that law, A -> P, with no
  k2.  The two answers for n_P differ by the I that is in transit: measured
  with these numbers the largest gap is 0.95 % of the charge.  For a
  closed system the difference in P is the amount of I itself, so the
  gap and the largest n_I are the same number.  The EduTool page changes
  k2 and draws it.

  THE SAME EQUATION AS TWO TANKS.  The gap here, at k1/k2 = eps, is the
  gap between two mixing tanks in series at tau_f/tau_s = eps
  (unsteady07/unsteady08): eps^(1/(1-eps)) in both, because both are a
  fast first-order system driven by a slow one.

  No species carries formation data and none is needed: isothermal mode
  holds T, so the heat of reaction is not used, as in batch03_consecutive.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        batchReactor;

        operation
        {
            mode         isothermal;
            T_setpoint   350.0 K;
        }

        reactions  ( A_to_I  I_to_P );
    }
);
